{
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "req_id": "REQ-003",
  "verdict": "salvageable",
  "summary": "The candidate implements the main REQ-003 intake flow with real service, router, adapter ports, and meaningful deny-path tests, but it is not yet hardening-ready. The most serious integrity issue is publish-before-commit behavior: a processing event can be emitted before the document transaction commits, which can leave downstream processing pointing at a rolled-back document. Promotion fit is also weakened by local redefinition of document and audit persistence instead of tighter reuse of canonical REQ-001 data ownership and shared audit seams.",
  "scope_fidelity": {
    "status": "partial",
    "notes": [
      "Core REQ-003 behavior is present: authenticated upload endpoint, size/type validation, storage adapter write, uploaded-state persistence, audit write, processing-request publication, and status query.",
      "Reject paths for unsupported type, oversize upload, missing multipart file, anonymous access, and unauthorized access are implemented with explicit client-visible reasons.",
      "Rollback on storage failure is implemented, but acceptance integrity is weakened by event publication occurring before database commit, so processing can be requested for a document that never commits.",
      "GET /documents/{documentId} is implemented, but it is guarded by the same upload permission dependency rather than a clearly separated read/status permission contract."
    ]
  },
  "source_substance": {
    "status": "mixed",
    "notes": [
      "This is not skeletal: there is real service logic, adapter wiring, repository code, request contracts, and runtime configuration.",
      "The main business logic is concentrated in service.py and is substantial enough to evaluate meaningfully.",
      "Repository ownership is weaker than expected for promotion because the candidate redefines Document and AuditEvent ORM rows locally instead of integrating more directly with canonical REQ-001 persistence contracts.",
      "Shared audit reuse expected by PLAN is effectively missing; audit persistence is embedded into the local repository rather than expressed through a clearer shared audit seam."
    ]
  },
  "test_substance": {
    "status": "partial",
    "notes": [
      "Tests cover the main accepted upload path and several important deny/failure paths, including storage failure and event publish failure cleanup.",
      "API tests assert real business outcomes such as persisted uploaded state, audit row creation, and exactly one published processing event in the fake publisher.",
      "Most tests are still fake-backed rather than true integration against SQLAlchemy persistence and configured adapters, so they do not prove canonical schema alignment or runtime wiring credibility.",
      "There is no test for the critical commit-failure inconsistency after successful event publication, and no evidence that the candidate protects downstream guarantees in that case."
    ]
  },
  "docs_code_coherence": {
    "status": "mixed",
    "notes": [
      "README, KIT, HOWTO, and LTC generally describe the emitted files and the implemented scope accurately.",
      "The docs do acknowledge the publish-before-commit gap rather than hiding it, which is honest and useful.",
      "However, the CI/docs package is richer than the proven implementation depth: LTC suggests stronger gate confidence than the fake-heavy test suite actually demonstrates.",
      "Operational examples depend on REQ-002 auth behavior that is not evidenced inside this candidate, so the examples are only conditionally runnable."
    ]
  },
  "promotion_fit": {
    "status": "partial",
    "notes": [
      "Module placement is broadly aligned with the planned canonical backend families and does not misuse runs/ as architecture.",
      "Shared storage and shared events ports are promotable abstractions and improve later reuse.",
      "Promotion risk remains elevated because the candidate duplicates canonical persistence structures locally and does not show a low-risk path to canonical REQ-001 ownership.",
      "The current transaction/event ordering is not safe enough for promotion hardening without first fixing the data/event consistency contract."
    ]
  },
  "shared_common_assessment": {
    "status": "mixed",
    "notes": [
      "Shared/common extraction for storage and events is justified by the REQ scope and later parity needs.",
      "There is no obvious over-extraction into generic shared modules.",
      "But shared/common correctness is incomplete because audit concerns expected to be reused are handled locally, and persistence structure is duplicated instead of leaning on canonical data ownership."
    ]
  },
  "dual_mode_assessment": {
    "status": "acceptable",
    "notes": [
      "The same business API and service flow are preserved across local, aws, and onprem modes through adapter/config selection rather than route branching.",
      "Using an S3-compatible adapter for local and onprem is directionally sound for promotion.",
      "On-prem eventing is only a provisional HTTP ingress seam, so dual-mode integrity is acceptable for a REQ-003 base but not parity-grade."
    ]
  },
  "blockers": [
    "Processing event publication happens before database commit, so a commit failure can leave downstream processing work for a non-persisted document.",
    "Persistence is locally redefined instead of clearly anchored to canonical REQ-001 data ownership, increasing promotion risk and drift risk.",
    "Test evidence is mostly fake-backed and does not validate canonical persistence integration or the critical commit-failure consistency gap."
  ],
  "hardening_targets": [
    "Harden the data/event consistency boundary so processing is not emitted for rolled-back uploads.",
    "Align intake persistence more tightly with canonical REQ-001 schema ownership and remove local schema drift risk.",
    "Introduce at least one real integration test using the emitted SQLAlchemy repository/session path rather than only fake unit-of-work fixtures.",
    "Add explicit tests for commit-failure behavior, status-query not-found behavior, and audit/persistence side-effect boundaries.",
    "Clarify and tighten read/status authorization semantics instead of reusing the upload permission guard for all document status access."
  ]
}
